    "section": "ARV|ELC_SHA|008|010422",
    "chunkType": "rule",
    "text": "ARV|ELC_SHA|008|010422\nPolicy incorporates the legislation titled The Sexual Harassment of Women at Workplace (Prevention,\nProhibition and Redressal) Act, (defined as “Act”) and its Rules (defined as “Rules”) notified and\npublished on 9th December 2013.\n2.\nProcedure:\nFor complaints raised by an aggrieved woman:\ni.\nHow to file a complaint: An Aggrieved Woman may lodge her complaint in writing or via e- mail\nwith the AIC as constituted under the Act. If the complaint is received via email, it should be\naffixed with signature to certify it as original / true copy.\nii.\nAnonymous complaints will not be entertained by the AIC\niii.\nThe Act provides protection against sexual harassment of women at the workplace. All complaints\nmade by Aggrieved Woman falling under the scope of this policy shall be dealt with by the AIC.\nThe parties shall not be allowed to bring in any legal practitioner to represent them in their\ncase at any stage of the proceedings before the AIC.\niv.\nWhen can an Aggrieved Woman file a complaint?\nAny Aggrieved Woman can file a complaint of sexual harassment, in writing to the AIC, within\na period of 3 (three) months from the date of incident or in case of a series of incidents, within\na period of 3 (three) months from the date of last incident.\nThis period may be extended for further 3 (three) months by the AIC for reasons to be recorded\nin writing if it is satisfied with the circumstance that prevented the Aggrieved Woman to file a\ncomplaint within the said period.\nv.\nConciliation by the AIC\nThe Aggrieved Woman may choose to adopt the informal process to resolve her\ncomplaint/experience of workplace sexual harassment. In such a case, AIC shall explore enabling\nways to address the complaint. This can include counselling, educating, orienting, or warning\nthe RE to promptly stop the unwelcome behaviour. At no point, the AIC will advise the\nAggrieved Woman to resolve the matter directly with the RE. Where such an informal process is\nsuccessful, such resolution must be recorded in the presence of both the Aggrieved Woman and\nthe RE.\nvi.\nInquiry Process of the AIC\na.\nAt the time of filing the complaint, the Aggrieved Woman shall submit to the AIC, six copies\nof the complaint along with supporting documents and the names and addresses of the\nwitnesses.\nb.\nOn receipt of the complaint, post a preliminary review, the AIC shall forward one copy\nof the complaint within 7 (seven) days to the RE for his response.\nc.\nThe RE must submit his response within 10 (ten) working days.\nd.\nPost receipt of the response, the AIC shall follow the principles of natural justice and\nconduct a thorough inquiry into the complaint giving both parties equal opportunityto\nmake representations and provide evidence.\ne.\nThe AIC can terminate an inquiry upon a notice of 15 (fifteen) days if the Aggrieved Woman,\nor the RE, fail to appear for 3 (three) consecutive proceedings conducted by the AIC.\nf.\nThe AIC shall have 90 (ninety) days to complete its inquiry.\nPrivate & Confidential\nOnly for Internal Circulation\nPage 7\nHR Policy\nClassification:\nInternal\nIssue Date:\n31.03.2022\nEffective From:\n01.05.2025\nPolicy Number:",
