    "section": "AIC COMMITTEE CONSTITUTION RULES",
    "chunkType": "rule",
    "text": "INTERNAL COMPLAINTS COMMITTEE (AIC) STRUCTURAL MANDATES:\nComposition Framework:\n1. Presiding Officer: A senior-level woman employee selected from Arvind management ranks.\n2. Internal Members: Minimum of 2 employees preferably committed to the cause of women, possessing social work background, or clear legal knowledge.\n3. External Member: Retained from an active NGO or professional association focused on women's rights, familiar with harassment remediation steps.\nREMOVAL TBD CONDITIONS:\nManagement holds rights to terminate an AIC member if they break confidentiality laws, abuse their position, get convicted of a legal offense, or face pending disciplinary inquiries. Vacancies must be filled via fresh nominations within 15 days of the event.\nOPERATING BUDGETS & CIVIL COURT POWERS:\n• External Member Payout: Paid a minimum remuneration of Rs. 250 per day plus standard conveyance travel allowances. Internal employees receive no extra stipend.\n• Legal Authority: The AIC is strictly independent of management and is explicitly vested with the same statutory powers as a Civil Court under the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908. They hold powers for summoning attendance, enforcing depositions, and ordering physical production of evidence documents.\n• Quorum SLAs: Inquiries require a minimum of 3 members physically present, including the Presiding Officer. The committee panel layout must maintain a minimum of 50% women representation for every single proceeding. No individual named as an active respondent can participate in committee operations. Regular member tenure is capped at 3 years or completion of 58 years of age (eligible for 1 additional re-nomination term).",
